    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Both tests pass and the build succeeds. The full test suite consists of `AcceptanceTest` (the other two files are an abstract base and a helper).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented owner soft-delete with minimal structural impact by adding small, cohesive guards rather than growing complex methods. Six files touched:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `deleted` field (`@Column(name=\"deleted\")`) plus a tiny `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted()` pair. A new owner defaults to `false` (primitive boolean).\n\n2. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.deleteOwner`** \u2014 swapped the hard `deleteOwner(owner)` for `owner.setDeleted(true); saveOwner(owner)`, still returning `204`. The record is retained and remains readable via `GET`.\n\n3. **`IdentityKey.rejectIfDuplicate`** \u2014 added a one-line `if (owner.isDeleted()) continue;` guard so the identity check skips deleted owners.\n\n4. **`DuplicateHouseholdException.rejectIfDuplicate`** \u2014 same one-line guard so the household-duplicate check skips deleted owners.\n\n5. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the read-only `deleted` boolean (default `false`) to the `Owner` response schema; MapStruct auto-maps it from the entity, so no mapper change was needed.\n\n6. **`db/hsqldb/schema.sql`** \u2014 added `deleted BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE` to the `owners` table (the test profile runs `ddl-auto=none`, so the column must exist; the `DEFAULT FALSE` keeps seed rows non-null for the primitive field).\n\nI deliberately kept the duplicate/identity filtering out of the large `addOwner` method and out of the big controller class, putting each guard in its own small utility class instead \u2014 and left the already-out-of-sync h2/mysql/postgres schemas alone (they omit many existing entity columns and rely on Hibernate, matching the project's existing convention).\n\nI did not commit anything, since this isn't a git repo and you didn't ask.",
